On XX/XX/ we received a text from WELLS FARGO BANK detecting fraud alert, asking if we authorized multiple charges from several businesses. The first charge of {$2100.00} to XXXX went through. We do not have any business with XXXX and never have, this was not our purchase. The next two charges that were attempted on our account were declined by Wells Fargo resulting in the fraud alert text mentioned above. The next day we see that the XXXX charge had in fact gone through. We called the Wells Fargo fraud department to file a claim assuming this charge would be reversed. To our dismay, Wells Fargo declined our fraud claim stating the " transaction was authorized '' ....? After going to our local Wells Fargo branch and speaking to our manager they suggested we submit the claim again and let them know we did not have this card in possession when the transaction occurred. Once again Wells Fargo declined our claim?

* We do not have an account with XXXX where the charge were made * We received a text from WELLS FARGO fraud alert suggesting it was fraud which we confirmed, and yet still have this charge to our account * This debit card had never been used for in-store purchases for the 4 years that we have had the account, it's only used for deposits. 

We have submitted this fraudulent claim to WELLS FARGO twice and been declined both times. WELLS FARGO insists that we made this transaction, which we did not! There is no justifiable reason why we have not received a credit to our account in the amount of {$2100.00}

On XXXX, I tried to pay my contractor {$2700.00}. 
I immediately contacted PayPal and expressed my concern that A ) the money was sent to the wrong person accidentally. PayPal told me to contact that person and ask for the money back. When I did that the person told me that his account was hacked XXXX years ago and he has no access to the PayPal account. So PayPal should not have allowed his account to remain active on the site and this would have prevented my mistake. 
B ) PayPal took out {$78.00} as a fee even though they advertised it as though there were no fees involved We are a non-profit and now we are out {$2700.00}

